# Package working areas — everything here is tracked (R5-A2, decided 2026-07-15).
#
# This file previously carried a blanket `*/*` rule that ignored the flat
# working area, on the theory that it is regenerable scratch. It is not:
#
#   refresh defines a MISSING working file as deletion of its generated output.
#   A clean clone has no working area, so the clone's first refresh reported
#   files_removed=[...] and deleted the compiler-visible source. Verified in a
#   scratch repo against this very file (R5-A2).
#
# The rule also silently swallowed anything created after it landed, because
# ignore rules never untrack what is already tracked. That is the only reason
# the existing sources survived — and it is how 15 design docs came to be
# tracked nowhere at all. Both failures share one cause: git's view and
# refresh's view of "does this file exist" disagreed.
#
# So the working area is now the source of truth and is committed alongside the
# generated patches/+files/. The cost is committed redundancy — a working file
# and its generated counterpart both live in git. That is deliberate: it makes
# refresh reproducible after a clone, which the alternative could not be
# without a rehydration design and an explicit deletion protocol.
#
# .pkgignore is unaffected. It stays exactly what it was: classification only,
# deciding what refresh emits into patches/+files/ — NOT what git tracks. It is
# what keeps design docs out of the distributable package, and it must never
# again double as a git policy.
#
# Deliberately empty of rules. Do not add a blanket working-area ignore back.
